        "page": 6,
        "label": "Page 6"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p007_b000",
      "title": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC\nCORE Safety Rules\n1� Rights and Responsibilities\nAll employees must make certain that:\n • Job tasks are performed only by individuals who are qualified and authorized. \n • Job briefings/task assessments are conducted before performing work and when \nactivity and/or conditions change. \n • We advise and redirect to safe work practices anyone observed performing an \nunsafe act. \n • We immediately inform each other of unsafe conditions, correct/protect it and \nreport it to a supervisor. \n • Accidents, incidents, including injuries, and related information are immediately \nreported to a supervisor. \n • Warning signs, posted instructions, placards, and/or barriers marking restricted \nareas and lockout tagout areas are displayed and complied with at all times. \n • We do not participate in horseplay or physical altercations while on Company \nproperty or participating in Company activities. \n • One person does not engage in work activity that by legislation or Company \npolicy requires two or more people. \n • A copy of the function specific safety rules must be accessible while on duty. \n • We do not possess or use a firearm on Company property unless authorized by  \nCPKC Police Services. \n2� Substance Abuse\n • Inappropriate substance use is prohibited as identified in CPKC Drug &  \nAlcohol Policies . \n • Do not smoke/use electronic cigarettes in company buildings, vehicles,  \nand machines.\n • For more information see CPKC HR Policy. \n3� Non-CPKC Personnel \nEmployees responsible for non-CPKC personnel must:\n • Understand the terms of the Minimum Safety Requirements for Contractors \nworking on CPKC property, follow access control procedures, and ensure they; \no  Receive a site-specific safety orientation and job briefing.\no  Wear PPE according to location-specific requirements. \no  Do not foul track without proper protection.\no  Have a Contractor Site Safety Plan/Emergency Information Sheet. \no  Have appropriate identification. \n4� Emergencies and Derailments\n • Follow all notification procedures as outlined in location-specific Emergency \nResponse Plans. \n • Approach an emergency or a derailment site only when safe to do so. \n5",
      "order": 6,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 7,
        "label": "Page 7"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p008_b000",
      "title": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\n • When arriving at an emergency site, contact the On-Scene Response Coordinator \nor Person-In-Charge for a job briefing and follow all procedures outlined in the \njob briefing. \n • Following an accident or incident, immediately notify a supervisor/dispatcher and \ndo not contact human remains, blood, or other fluids.\n5� Fire Safety\n • Maintain unrestricted access to, and be familiar with the location of, fire \nextinguishers, alarm boxes, exit aisles, circuit breakers, fuse panels, emergency \nexits and local emergency response plans.\n • Immediately replace or report any defective, discharged or missing fire \nextinguishers. \n • Store flammable and combustible material away from ignition sources.\n • When qualified, use proper grounding and bonding techniques to prevent static \nelectrical charge when dispensing or transferring flammable liquids. \n • Do not leave open flames unattended. \n • Dispose of flammable or combustible materials according to established \nCompany guidelines.\n • Upon discovery of a fire, if safe to do so, extinguish the fire. Employees must \nimmediately activate the fire alarm (where available), and promptly report the \nfire to supervisor, dispatcher and/or proper authority.\n6� Work Environment\n • Keep all work areas clean, orderly, and free from clutter and debris. \n • Must properly dispose of refuse in appropriate and approved receptacle or \nwaste bin. \n • Employees who use needles, lancets and medical sharps must properly dispose of \nthem in sharps containers, if provided, or remove them from company property \nfor disposal, if not provided.  Do not throw needles, lancets, and medical sharps \nin company trash.\n • Use approved products to wash equipment, clothing, or floors. \n • Do not use compressed air or gases to remove debris from body or clothing. \n • Do not walk under suspended loads. \n • Work materials must be kept neatly and not stored above shoulder height unless \nthe area is designed for palletized equipment/materials with a specified load \nlimit and a forklift is utilized.\n • Walkways must be kept clear to eliminate tripping hazards. \n7� Vehicles, Materials, Equipment, and Tools\n • Operate, maintain, and repair vehicles, equipment, tools, and materials only \nwhen qualified, properly licensed and authorized. \n • Use CSA B376 or equivalent UL / ULC (CAN) or Department of Transportation \n(DOT) (US/Mexico) approved, properly marked containers when storing or \ntransporting flammable liquids or materials.\n • Use approved tools, equipment, and materials for the purpose(s) intended. \n • Do not modify or override safety devices or remove safety guards. \n6",
      "order": 7,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 8,
        "label": "Page 8"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p009_b000",
      "title": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC\n • Maintain, repair, and store equipment according to prescribed instructions. \n • Inspect all vehicles, equipment, tools, and related safety devices for unsafe \nconditions before use. \n • Repair or tag and remove from service if defective. \n • Use three-point contact when getting on or off any equipment, machinery, \nladder, or vehicle. \n • Face the vehicle or equipment/track unit when getting on or off. \n • Ventilate exhaust when operating internal-combustion equipment inside \nbuildings or enclosed structures. \n8. Office Safety\n • When sitting in a chair, keep all chair legs or casters on the floor. \n • When not in use, chairs shall be kept pushed in to keep walkways and \nworkspaces clear. \n • Roller style chairs are only allowed at workstations, training, and  \nmeeting/office rooms. \n • Fixed leg chairs are required at ready room and lunch tables. \n • Use only commercially available stepstools that meet CSA standards (CAN)/ANSI \nStandards (US/Mexico) to access elevated locations.\n • Ensure that desk and file cabinet doors and drawers are closed when not in use. \n • When ascending or descending flights of stairs employees must utilize handrails. \n9� Use of Electronic Devices\nFor Employees governed by GCOR/OTS or CROR refer to the applicable operating rules \nand related system special instructions� \nFor employees not governed by GCOR/OTS or CROR refer to the CPKC Use of Electronic \nDevices Procedure for applicable instructions. \n7",
      "order": 8,
      "parent_id": null,
